thread:
omar: Heads up — our .NET sample app spawns 4 worker processes and we hit tenant-level 429s even though each process has a client-side concurrency limiter set to 6. Seems like the per-process limiter isn't coordinating across processes so aggregate inflight > quota. Anyone else seen multi-process leakage?

lee: how are you launching the workers? dotnet worker processes or dotnet threads?

omar: separate OS processes (dotnet run x4) to simulate multi-core load. each process creates its own InferenceClient with internal leaky-semaphore. sample snippet:

```csharp
// simplified
var client = new RedwoodClient(token);
var limiter = new ConcurrencyLimiter(6); // process-local
foreach(var item in queue){
  await limiter.WaitAsync();
  _ = Task.Run(async () => {
    try { await client.GenerateAsync(item); }
    finally { limiter.Release(); }
  });
}
```

ops: server headers you saw? any x-tenant-rate or similar?

omar: headers from failure: x-ratelimit-tenant-limit: 240, x-ratelimit-tenant-remaining: 0, x-ratelimit-reset: 2712345900, x-client-session: dotnet:proc_3

gita: so 4 processes * 6 concurrency = 24 inflight but tenant limit is 240 — that shouldn't hit tenant limit unless many more requests or retries. Are you also doing aggressive prefetch/paging?

omar: yes we prefetched pages aggressively across workers, and when some workers hit transient errors they retry immediately (no centralized backoff). result: short bursts of retries + prefetch made aggregate token/sec high and tenant counter dropped to 0.

lee: two things: 1) client-side limiting needs to be coordinated when you run multi-process (use a shared token bucket via IPC/Redis/file or reduce per-process limit); 2) make retries respect Retry-After and include jitter and a global process-level cooldown when 429s observed.

omar: tried lowering per-process limiter to 2 — reduced frequency but still saw spikes during startup when all processes prefetch simultaneously.

gita: consider leader-elected prefetcher: one process does warmup/prefetch and others wait for a signal, or use randomized delayed startups so prefetch windows don't align. also set maxRetries and exponential backoff for paging requests.

ops: from infra side, we should expose a tenant-level token bucket metric so customers can see aggregated usage in console. filing infra ticket to discuss (TKT-3819).

dx-bot: quick devdoc suggestion: add a note in the .NET sampleapp README about multi-process deployments: coordinate concurrency limits, prefer a single warmup process or shared IPC bucket, honor Retry-After on 429, and add jittered prefetch windows. I'll draft a short recipe.

omar: another subtlety — the .NET client reinitializes the HTTP connection pool on token refresh which caused temporary new connections and duplicate warmup requests across processes. keeping a shared persistent HttpClient or reusing sockets per process helped.

lee: +1 on reusing HttpClient. also log x-tenant-rate headers and surface them in sampleapp telemetry so engineers can tune limits during onboarding.

gita: I'll prototype an example using Redis for a cross-process semaphore for the sampleapp; will post a PR with code and benchmark results.

omar: thanks all — I'll add backoff + leader prefetch and re-run CI. will drop logs here once I have a repro. :thumbsup:
